<!-- @opening -->
<p class="story-lead">A shared checkout screen renders correctly on both phones. On iOS, a native map inside it captures the wrong gesture and the keyboard obscures a field. Sharing composables reduced duplicate UI code; it did not settle the host’s interaction contract.</p>

Follow one shared surface through its native host, navigation and acceptance evidence.

<!-- @after 1. Choose the UI-Sharing Boundary Deliberately :: visual -->
### One surface, explicit owners

<figure class="viz"><table><caption>Treat checkout as one bounded shared surface</caption><tbody><tr><th scope="row">1</th><td>Native host: presentation, integration and top-level route</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">2</th><td>Shared checkout: internal state and steps</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">3</th><td>Native capability: explicit interop boundary</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">4</th><td>Completion event: one navigation owner consumes it</td></tr></tbody></table><figcaption>The boundaries in this example are different responsibilities, not implied packet transfers.</figcaption></figure>

A native host can embed a Compose surface, and Compose can host native UI through supported integration APIs. That permits a bounded checkout flow without requiring every platform screen to migrate at once. Give the flow a small input such as an order identifier and a semantic outcome such as completion or cancellation. In this design the native coordinator owns top-level navigation; the shared flow owns its internal steps.

Walk through keyboard entry, scrolling over an embedded native control, accessibility focus, back navigation and returning after interruption on each shipped target. Correct pixels are only one part of that journey. When checkout completes, send its outcome through the one chosen coordinator rather than independently updating two top-level stacks. Another product can choose a shared stack, but it still needs one canonical owner for restoration and back behavior. Keep platform acceptance evidence beside the shared screen’s release decision.

<!-- @after 3. Navigation: One Stack Owner per Surface :: animation -->
### Leave a shared flow through its owner

<figure class="stepper" data-illustrated data-no-handoff><span class="stepper-title">Leave a shared flow through its owner</span><div class="stage viz-cols" data-cols="2"><div class="col"><span class="col-title">Contract and use</span><div class="node" data-el="n0" data-art="phone" data-art-role="client"><strong>Native host</strong><small data-text="v0">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="n1" data-art="screen" data-art-role="client"><strong>Shared flow</strong><small data-text="v1">—</small></div></div><div class="col"><span class="col-title">Platform and ownership</span><div class="node" data-el="n2" data-art="signal" data-art-role="runtime"><strong>Semantic event</strong><small data-text="v2">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="n3" data-art="graph" data-art-role="runtime"><strong>Navigation owner</strong><small data-text="v3">—</small></div></div></div><script type="application/json" class="steps">[{"label": "Enter", "caption": "The platform coordinator opens a bounded shared checkout flow with an order ID. It owns top-level navigation in this illustrative design.", "add": ["n0", "n1", "n2", "n3"], "state": {"n0": "active", "n1": "idle", "n2": "idle", "n3": "idle"}, "text": {"v0": "Opens surface", "v1": "Receives identifier", "v2": "None yet", "v3": "Native coordinator"}}, {"label": "Interact", "caption": "The flow owns only its internal steps. Platform capabilities stay behind explicit seams.", "add": ["n0", "n1", "n2", "n3"], "state": {"n0": "idle", "n1": "active", "n2": "idle", "n3": "idle"}, "text": {"v0": "Hosts the flow", "v1": "Internal state/stack", "v2": "None yet", "v3": "No mirrored top-level stack"}}, {"label": "Complete", "caption": "The shared screen reports a semantic completion event. It does not also push an independent top-level native destination.", "add": ["n0", "n1", "n2", "n3"], "state": {"n0": "idle", "n1": "idle", "n2": "active", "n3": "idle"}, "text": {"v0": "Receives completion", "v1": "Reports outcome", "v2": "Checkout finished", "v3": "One event path"}}, {"label": "Navigate", "caption": "The coordinator applies navigation once and tests back/restoration. Other products can choose a shared stack if that ownership is equally explicit.", "add": ["n0", "n1", "n2", "n3"], "state": {"n0": "idle", "n1": "idle", "n2": "idle", "n3": "active"}, "text": {"v0": "Updates product flow", "v1": "Surface can end", "v2": "Consumed by owner", "v3": "Single canonical decision"}}]</script><figcaption>Illustrative sequence, not a measured trace. <a href="https://kotlinlang.org/docs/multiplatform/compose-swiftui-integration.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Primary contract</a>.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Common Failure Modes :: check -->
### Try the decision yourself

<details><summary>Does shared Compose mean UIKit widgets everywhere?</summary><p>No. Shared programming APIs and native host/accessibility integration do not imply identical native widget implementations.</p></details>
<details><summary>Should native and shared stacks mirror every push?</summary><p>Give each visible stack segment one owner and translate semantic events at the seam.</p></details>
<details><summary>What proves the screen is ready?</summary><p>Representative target-device evidence for required input, accessibility, restoration, performance and integrations.</p></details>

